Object.assign(CHAPTER_EN, {
  c16: { title: "Chapter 16: Counting, Filtering and Statistics", theory: "Common problem forms: counting elements that satisfy a condition, filtering numbers, and conditional sums over a list or a range.", objectives: ["Count and filter list elements under a condition", "Compute basic statistics: sum, average, maximum, minimum", "Combine filtering and statistics in one problem"] },
  c17: { title: "Chapter 17: Geometry and Practical Formulas", theory: "Apply mathematics formulas in code: circles, triangles, unit conversions and simple electricity bills.", objectives: ["Turn geometry and practical formulas into code", "Check units and the order of operations in a formula", "Round and display results as required"] },
  c18: { title: "Chapter 18: Synthesis Practice for Students", theory: "Review combining if, for, lists and f-strings in the style of class tests. How to do a synthesis problem: read the problem carefully and write down the output you need to print first; identify the given data (variables, lists); choose the right technique (if for branching, for for looping, f-string for pretty printing); write each small part and press Run to check before submitting. Common mistakes: printing lines in the wrong order, extra or missing spaces in strings, and forgetting to update counter and total variables inside loops.", objectives: ["Review all problem forms for students before a test", "Choose the right solving strategy for each problem form", "Manage test time: do easy problems first, hard ones later"] },
  c19: { title: "Chapter 19: Advanced Functions (University)", theory: "Write functions that take parameters and return values, combined with if and for — the foundation of university lab exercises.", objectives: ["Write advanced functions: default parameters and multiple return values", "Understand local and global variable scope", "Split code into functions so it can be reused"] },
  c20: { title: "Chapter 20: Classic Algorithms (University)", theory: "Foundational algorithms: greatest common divisor, array sums, list reversal and linear search — common in early data-structure labs.", objectives: ["Write basic classic algorithms: search and simple sorting", "Walk through each algorithm step with a small example", "Test an algorithm with boundary data"] },
  c21: { title: "Chapter 21: Extra Practice · Extended Basics", theory: "Review print, variables, if and for with everyday situations: shopping money, attendance and schedules.", objectives: ["Strengthen basic knowledge with extra practice", "Write correct code faster on the first try", "Recognize which problem form you are still weak at and practise it again"] },
  c22: { title: "Chapter 22: Extra Practice · Light Algorithms", theory: "Review exercises: number sequences, counting and nested-loop simulation — a bridge between strong school students and first-year university students.", objectives: ["Practise more light algorithms: counting, finding and simple sorting", "Recognize repeated problem patterns across many problems", "Optimize code: remove useless loops and use the right structure"] },
  c23: { title: "Chapter 23: Dictionaries and Table Data", theory: "A dictionary, or dict, stores data as key-value pairs in curly braces. You get a value by its key in square brackets, add or change a key by assignment, and use len to count keys. A list of dictionaries is a data table: each dictionary is a row, each key is a column, and you can loop over rows with for to compute sums, averages or count rows that meet a condition.", objectives: ["Create and look up dictionaries by key", "Store table data with dicts and lists combined", "Count frequencies and statistics with dicts"] },
  c24: { title: "Chapter 24: Advanced Functions and Recursion", theory: "A function does not only return a number: it can return a list or a dict carrying many results, and a function can call another function to split work up. Recursion is when a function calls itself: each call makes the problem smaller, and there must be a stopping condition, or the function will call itself forever. Common mistakes are forgetting the stopping condition, forgetting return in the recursive branch, or calling a function with the wrong number of arguments.", objectives: ["Write recursive functions with a clear base case", "Trace recursion with a small example", "Recognize when recursion fits and when it does not"] },
  c25: { title: "Chapter 25: Basic Search and Sorting", theory: "Linear search checks each element until it finds the wanted value — simple but slow for long lists. Binary search is faster but requires a sorted list: each time it compares with the middle element and discards one half. Sorting algorithms all rely on comparing and swapping, and common mistakes are indices outside the list or forgetting to swap both sides.", objectives: ["Write linear search and basic sorting algorithms", "Compare the number of steps of different search and sorting methods", "Test algorithms on sorted and unsorted lists"] },
  c26: { title: "Chapter 26: Advanced Strings and Text Data", theory: "Python strings have many methods: split breaks a string into a list of words, join joins a list into one string, strip removes extra spaces at both ends, lower and upper change case, replace replaces a substring and count counts occurrences. Common mistakes are forgetting that these methods return a new string instead of changing the original, or splitting a string without handling extra spaces, which makes word counts wrong.", objectives: ["Process advanced strings: split, join, find and replace", "Clean text data before computing statistics", "Count words, count characters and find patterns in text"] },
  c27: { title: "Chapter 27: Two-Dimensional Lists and Matrices", theory: "A two-dimensional list, or matrix, is a list whose elements are lists: element bang[i][j] sits in row i and column j, both starting at 0. To visit the whole table, use two nested loops for rows and columns, and to build a new table, create each empty row and append values into it. Common mistakes are confusing rows with columns when transposing, or using an index outside the table.", objectives: ["Create and traverse two-dimensional lists and matrices", "Compute row sums, column sums and diagonals", "Check row and column indices to avoid out-of-range errors"] },
  c28: { title: "Chapter 28: Small Student Management Project", theory: "This project combines everything learned into one data table: each student is a dict with name, class and score, and the whole class is a list of dicts. To make a report, loop over dicts, add scores to compute averages, compare to find the highest score, count by condition to filter passing students, and count by class. Common mistakes are using the wrong dict key, or forgetting to initialize counter and maximum variables before the loop.", objectives: ["Build a small student management project with lists and dicts", "Split a project into features: add, edit, delete, find, statistics", "Test a project with sample user actions"] },
  c29: { title: "Chapter 29: Advanced Logic and Control", theory: "One condition can combine several ideas with and (both must be true), or (only one needs to be true) and not (reverses). break stops a loop entirely as soon as a condition is met, while continue only skips the current iteration and moves to the next. Common mistakes are writing and with an index that can go outside the list, forgetting break so a loop runs through the whole list, or confusing break with continue: break exits entirely, continue only skips one iteration.", objectives: ["Combine nested branches and nested loops", "Write complex conditions with and, or, not in a clear way", "Debug logic by running each case"] },
  c30: { title: "Chapter 30: Reading Code, Guessing Results and Fixing Bugs", theory: "Reading code is a separate skill from writing code: you must run each line in your head, track how variables change, and only then guess the result. Fixing bugs is the same: find the exact wrong line before fixing it — a missing colon, = instead of ==, forgotten indentation, a wrong condition, or a forgotten counter increase in while. An honest note: the grader in this system only compares printed output and required techniques, so bug-fixing exercises ask you to rewrite the correct code, while result-guessing is also asked in the chapter test's multiple-choice questions.", objectives: ["Read code and guess its result before running it", "Find and fix logic errors in existing code", "Explain why code is wrong instead of only fixing it until it runs"] },
  c31: { title: "Chapter 31: Table Data Reports", theory: "Group reports are built with dictionaries: the key is the group name, the value is the count, total or maximum of that group. For each row, take the group name and accumulate into the dict with get(group, 0) so a missing group still starts at 0, and use get(key, 0) so a row missing a key does not break the report. Finally print only the groups in the fixed order given in the problem — do not print the whole dict, or the order will be off. Common mistakes are overwriting instead of accumulating, or forgetting to initialize a new group.", objectives: ["Summarize table data into reports", "Compute sums, averages and ratios by group", "Present reports as easy-to-read tables"] },
  c32: { title: "Chapter 32: Basic Artificial Intelligence (CS311)", theory: "Artificial intelligence is not about writing every rule in advance, but about letting a machine learn patterns from data: training data to learn a model, test data to measure accuracy, and never using test data for training. This chapter hand-writes every algorithm with lists and loops, without ready-made libraries, in the spirit of CS311: k-NN by nearest points, perceptron adjusting weights when it guesses wrong, and linear regression finding the line that best fits the data. A common mistake is memorizing the training data and forgetting to split off a test set.", objectives: ["Explain what artificial intelligence can and cannot do", "Tell data, models and predictions apart", "Write a Python program illustrating a basic AI idea"] },
  c33: { title: "Chapter 33: Data Structures", theory: "A stack is last-in first-out, a queue is first-in first-out, a hash table (dict) looks up by key, a tree is a dict nested inside a dict, and a graph is a dict where each vertex maps to a list of neighbours. Breadth-first search (BFS) uses a queue, depth-first search (DFS) uses recursion. Common mistakes are calling pop() on an empty list, forgetting to mark visited vertices so BFS loops forever, and confusing the entry and exit order of stacks with queues.", objectives: ["Choose the right data structure: list, dict, set, stack, queue", "Compare the advantages of each structure in each situation", "Write code that uses a data structure to solve a specific problem"] },
  c34: { title: "Chapter 34: Advanced Algorithms", theory: "Quick sort picks a pivot and splits a list into smaller and larger groups, binary search only works when the list is sorted, dynamic programming stores already-computed subresults in a dict so they are not computed again, and two pointers and sliding windows let list problems be solved in a single pass. Common mistakes are binary searching an unsorted list, forgetting the recursion stopping condition, and recomputing the same subproblem many times instead of storing it in a table.", objectives: ["Write advanced algorithms and analyze their number of steps", "Recognize problems that need efficient sorting or searching", "Test algorithms with larger data"] },
  c35: { title: "Chapter 35: Advanced Recursion and Divide and Conquer", theory: "Divide and conquer splits a large problem into identical but smaller subproblems, solves them, and merges the results: quick sort, binary search and fast exponentiation all work this way. Backtracking tries one choice, and if it fails, goes back and tries another. Every recursion needs a clear stopping condition and subproblems that keep getting smaller, or it will call itself forever or overflow the stack. Common mistakes are forgetting the base case, and slicing with wrong bounds so the subproblem does not shrink.", objectives: ["Write advanced recursion and divide and conquer", "Identify the base case and the step that shrinks the problem", "Track recursion depth to avoid errors"] },
  c36: { title: "Chapter 36: Object-Oriented Programming Simulation", theory: "This chapter practises object-oriented thinking with dicts and functions, as preparation before learning real class syntax in the Object-Oriented Programming lesson: an object is a dict holding fields, a method is a function that takes that dict as an argument, and a constructor is a function that returns a new dict. Inheritance is simulated by copying a parent object's fields and adding new ones, and polymorphism is simulated by choosing behaviour by object type. After this chapter, moving to real Python only means changing dicts to classes and functions to methods — the thinking stays the same. Common mistakes are forgetting that a function must receive the exact object it should change, and thinking that copying a dict creates a new object when it is only a reference.", objectives: ["Model objects with classes: attributes and methods", "Create many objects from one class and let them interact", "Separate an object's data and behaviour clearly"] },
  c37: { title: "Chapter 37: Advanced Synthesis Project", theory: "The advanced project combines many skills into one program: keyword search, similarity suggestions, grouping then sorting groups by total, shortest paths with BFS, word frequency analysis, revenue by product, and a cache so nothing is computed twice. Each exercise starts from given sample data, writes code to compute the result, and prints exactly the format the problem asks for. Common project mistakes are getting each small part right but forgetting to combine them into the final answer, and printing a whole structure instead of printing exactly the lines the problem needs.", objectives: ["Complete an advanced synthesis project from an open-ended requirement", "Plan: split a project into modules and test each part", "Present and verify a project with real running examples"] },
  c38: { title: "Chapter 38: Introductory AI Thinking — Data, Features and Labels", theory: "Every AI system starts from data. One data sample consists of features — numbers or words describing a thing — and possibly a label, the correct answer we want the machine to predict. Before teaching a machine, we must count, clean, normalize data and split it into a training set (for learning) and a test set (for measuring). The most important thinking habit of an AI practitioner is always to ask: what does this data say, what is missing, and where is it biased. Common mistakes are mixing labels into features and using test data for training.", objectives: ["Explain data, features and labels in machine learning", "Prepare simple table data for an AI problem", "Recognize how bad data affects results"] },
  c39: { title: "Chapter 39: Rule-Based Systems and Expert Systems", theory: "Before machine learning, AI was mostly rule systems: experts wrote rules in IF condition THEN conclusion form, and the machine just applied them. Rules are easy to understand, explain and control, but hard to scale because the world has too many exceptions, and rules do not improve on their own when new data arrives. This chapter practises turning knowledge into if/elif, combining conditions with and/or, filtering by keywords, scoring rules and measuring how often a rule set is right. Common mistakes are ordering rules wrong (a broad rule standing before a narrow one blocks it) and writing = instead of ==.", objectives: ["Write rule-based systems: if-then rules for decisions", "Describe expert knowledge as clear rules", "Test a rule set with conflicting and missing-data cases"] },
  c40: { title: "Chapter 40: Measuring and Evaluating Models", theory: "An AI model is only valuable when we can measure how good it is. For classification, we count four outcome types: TP (predicted positive, correct), TN (predicted negative, correct), FP (predicted positive, wrong) and FN (predicted negative, wrong). From these we compute accuracy, precision (of the predicted positives, how many are correct), recall (of the truly positive cases, how many are caught) and F1. Always compare with a naive model that always predicts the majority class, because with skewed data, high accuracy can be an illusion. Measure on the test set: a model at 100% in training but low in testing is memorizing (overfitting). Common mistakes are dividing by zero and looking only at accuracy.", objectives: ["Measure a model with basic metrics: correct, wrong, accuracy rate", "Tell training sets and test sets apart", "Explain why a model correct on training data can still be wrong in practice"] },
  c41: { title: "Chapter 41: Hand-Written Classical Machine Learning", theory: "This chapter implements classic machine learning algorithms by hand, using only loops and lists, to understand their nature. k-NN predicts a label by voting among the k nearest points. A centroid classifier compares a new point with the average of each class. Linear regression finds the line that best fits the data, and a loss function (such as the sum of squared errors) measures the deviation. Gradient descent is how a machine learns: repeatedly stepping against the slope of the loss function with a step size lr (learning rate). A perceptron corrects its weights each time it guesses wrong. k-means groups data without labels by repeatedly assigning points to the nearest centre and recomputing centres. A trick in these exercises: because pairs cannot be sorted yet, we encode [distance, label] as one number distance * 10 + label so it can be sorted. Common mistakes: an lr that is too large makes gradient descent jump around, and forgetting to update cluster centres.", objectives: ["Hand-write a simple classical machine learning model", "Train a model from sample data and try new predictions", "Recognize the limits of a hand-written model compared with a library"] },
  c42: { title: "Chapter 42: Language, Text and Recommender Systems", theory: "A machine does not understand words, it only works with numbers, so the first step of language processing is turning text into numbers. The simplest way is a bag of words: split into words, count frequencies, and represent each text as a count vector over a fixed vocabulary. The more similar two vectors are, the larger their dot product (or cosine), so we use it to find texts with similar meaning, find users with similar taste, and recommend products. Naive Bayes uses counted frequencies to estimate label probabilities, and Markov chains predict the next word from a table counting word pairs. Common mistakes: forgetting to normalize upper and lower case before counting, and using different vocabularies for two texts that need to be compared.", objectives: ["Process text simply for a language problem", "Build simple suggestions from frequency and similarity", "Judge suggestions with concrete examples instead of feeling"] },
  c43: { title: "Chapter 43: Search, Planning and Decision Making", theory: "Classical AI solves problems by searching through a space of states. A graph describes states and steps between them. BFS (breadth-first search, using a queue) always gives the path with the fewest steps on an unweighted graph, DFS (depth-first, using recursion or a stack) goes deep to the end and then backtracks. A greedy algorithm picks the best-looking option at each step — fast but not always optimal. A heuristic estimates the remaining distance, for example Manhattan distance. Minimax for two-player games: MAX chooses the move that maximizes the score, MIN chooses the one that minimizes it. Hill climbing improves a solution step by step and can get stuck at a local peak. Reinforcement learning (Q-learning) updates Q values from rewards. Common mistakes: forgetting to mark visited vertices, causing infinite loops, and checking grid bounds wrong.", objectives: ["Write simple search and planning algorithms", "Describe a problem's states, actions and goal", "Compare search strategies by number of steps"] },
  c44: { title: "Chapter 44: Responsible AI and Synthesis Project", theory: "A model that performs well on average can still be unfair to a small group. A responsible AI practitioner checks whether data is skewed, measures accuracy separately for each group, compares approval rates between groups, avoids leakage (test samples slipping into training, making scores illusory), hides personal information, sets safety guardrails for outputs, and hands uncertain cases to humans for review. A decision threshold is also an ethical choice, because the two kinds of errors have different costs. The final chapter combines every step into a complete pipeline: prepare data, split sets, train, evaluate, and then conclude whether the model should be used. A common mistake: reporting only the average number and believing that a high score means a fair model.", objectives: ["Recognize bias, privacy and risks when using AI", "Judge an AI application from a responsibility angle", "Complete a synthesis project with verification and explained results"] },
});
