export const LESSON_FULL_EN = {"l01":{"why":"Every program needs to show results to the user. print() is the simplest way for a program to 'talk' to you, and it is also the number-one tool for checking whether code is running correctly.","concept":"A Python program runs line by line, from top to bottom.\n\nprint(...) prints what is inside the parentheses. Text (strings) must be in quotes \"...\" or '...'. Numbers are written directly, with no quotes.\n\nWhen you put many values in print, separated by commas, Python inserts one space between them.\n\nA line starting with # is a comment: Python ignores it, it is only there so readers understand the code.","syntax":"print(\"text to print\")\nprint(value_1, value_2)\n# this is a comment","walkthrough":["Line 1: a comment, Python ignores it.","Line 2: prints the string Xin chào Python.","Line 3: prints two values, a string and a number; Python adds a space in between.","Line 4: Python computes 3 + 4 first, then prints the result 7."],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"print(Xin chào)","why":"Missing quotes, Python thinks Xin is a variable name and reports NameError or SyntaxError.","fix":"print(\"Xin chào\")"},{"wrong":"Print(\"Xin chào\")","why":"Python is case-sensitive. Print is not print.","fix":"print(\"Xin chào\")"},{"wrong":"print(\"Xin chào\"","why":"Missing closing parenthesis.","fix":"print(\"Xin chào\")"}],"summary":["print() prints values to the screen, one line per print call.","Strings go in quotes, numbers do not.","A comma inside print inserts one space.","# starts a comment."],"practice":[{"id":"l01p1","title":"A two-line greeting","prompt":"Print exactly 2 lines:\nTôi đang học Python\nHọc từ con số 0"},{"id":"l01p2","title":"Print a calculation","prompt":"Print the line: Tổng là 15 (Python must compute 7 + 8 itself, do not type 15 directly)."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does print(\"A\", \"B\") print?","options":["AB","A B","A,B","\"A\" \"B\""]},{"q":"Which line is a comment?","options":["// note","# note","<!-- note -->","/* note */"]},{"q":"What does print(2 * 3) print?","options":["2 * 3","6","23","Error"]}]},"l02":{"why":"A program needs to remember information: a user name, a score, a price. A variable is a 'labelled box' for storing and retrieving that information.","concept":"Write ten_bien = gia_tri to create a variable. The = in Python means assignment (storing a value in the box), not 'equals'.\n\nFour basic types: int is an integer (10, -3), float is a real number (3.14, 5.0), str is a text string (\"Lan\"), bool is true/false (True, False).\n\nA variable name contains letters, digits and _, does not start with a digit, and has no spaces. Use meaningful names like diem_toan instead of x.\n\nAssigning a new value replaces the old one.","syntax":"ten = \"Lan\"\ntuoi = 15\nchieu_cao = 1.55\nda_dang_ky = True\nprint(type(tuoi))","walkthrough":["Line 1: create the variable ten holding the string \"Lan\".","Line 2: create the variable tuoi holding the integer 15.","Line 3: take the old value 15, add 1, and assign it back into tuoi; now tuoi is 16.","Line 4: print the values, separated by spaces.","Line 5: type(ten) tells you ten has type str."],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"2ten = \"Lan\"","why":"A variable name cannot start with a digit.","fix":"ten2 = \"Lan\""},{"wrong":"ho ten = \"Lan\"","why":"A variable name has no spaces.","fix":"ho_ten = \"Lan\""},{"wrong":"print(Tuoi)","why":"You created tuoi (lowercase) but called Tuoi: Python reports NameError.","fix":"print(tuoi)"}],"summary":["= is assignment.","int, float, str, bool are the 4 basic types.","type() tells you the type of a value.","Variable names are case-sensitive, so pick meaningful names."],"practice":[{"id":"l02p1","title":"Introduce yourself","prompt":"Create variables ten = \"Minh\" and tuoi = 12. Then print: Minh 12 tuổi (use the variables, do not type it directly)."},{"id":"l02p2","title":"The type of a value","prompt":"Create a variable gia = 9.5. Print the type of gia with type()."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What type is the value 5.0?","options":["int","float","str","bool"]},{"q":"Which variable name is valid?","options":["diem toan","1diem","diem_toan","diem-toan"]},{"q":"After x = 3 then x = x * 2, what is x?","options":["3","5","6","x * 2"]}]},"l03":{"why":"A useful program must take data from the user: enter a name, a score, an amount of money. input() does this.","concept":"input() reads one line the user types and ALWAYS returns a string (str), even when they type a number.\n\nTo compute, convert to a number: int(input()) for integers, float(input()) for real numbers.\n\nDivision / always gives a float (7 / 2 = 3.5, 6 / 2 = 3.0). // is floor division (7 // 2 = 3), % is the remainder (7 % 2 = 1).\n\nOn Python Lab, input data comes from the \"Input data\" box, one line per input() call.","syntax":"ten = input()\nn = int(input())\nx = float(input())","walkthrough":["Line 1: read the first line \"7\", convert it to the number 7, assign to a.","Line 2: read the second line \"2\", convert it to the number 2.","Line 3: 7 + 2 = 9.","Line 4: 7 / 2 = 3.5 (always a real number).","Line 5: 7 divided by 2 leaves remainder 1."],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"a = input()\nprint(a + 1)","why":"a is a string, it cannot be added to a number: TypeError.","fix":"a = int(input())"},{"wrong":"a = input()\nb = input()\nprint(a + b)  # enter 2 and 3","why":"Adding two strings concatenates them, printing 23 instead of 5.","fix":"Use int(input()) for both."},{"wrong":"n = int(input())  # user types 2.5","why":"int() cannot convert a string with a dot: ValueError.","fix":"Use float(input()) when the data can be a real number."}],"summary":["input() always returns a string.","int(), float() convert strings to numbers.","/ gives a real number, // gives the integer part, % gives the remainder."],"practice":[{"id":"l03p1","title":"Greet by name","prompt":"Read a name from the keyboard, then print: Xin chào <name>!\nFor example, entering An prints: Xin chào An!"},{"id":"l03p2","title":"Rectangle perimeter","prompt":"Read the length and width (two integers, one per line). Print the perimeter = (length + width) * 2."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What type does input() return?","options":["int","float","str","Depends on the data typed"]},{"q":"How much is 9 // 4?","options":["2.25","2","1","3"]},{"q":"What does 8 / 2 print?","options":["4","4.0","4.00","Error"]}]},"l04":{"why":"A smart program must branch: enough points means pass, too few means fail; the right password gets in, the wrong one gets an error.","concept":"if dieu_kien: runs the indented block below when the condition is true.\n\nelif (else if) checks the next condition when the earlier ones are false. else runs when all are false. Python stops at the FIRST true branch.\n\nComparison uses ==, while = is assignment. Combine conditions: and (both true), or (one of them true), not (negation).\n\nAfter the : you must go to a new line and indent 4 spaces. Lines in the same block must be indented equally.","syntax":"if dieu_kien_1:\n    lenh\nelif dieu_kien_2:\n    lenh\nelse:\n    lenh","walkthrough":["Line 1: read the score 6.5.","Line 2: 6.5 >= 8 is false, skip line 3.","Line 4: 6.5 >= 5 is true, run line 5 printing Đạt.","Lines 6-7: do not run, because a branch was already true."],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"if x = 5:","why":"= is assignment; comparison must use ==.","fix":"if x == 5:"},{"wrong":"if x > 3\n    print(x)","why":"Missing the colon at the end of the if line.","fix":"if x > 3:"},{"wrong":"if x > 3:\nprint(x)","why":"The block after if must be indented: IndentationError.","fix":"Add 4 spaces before print(x)."}],"summary":["if / elif / else for branching.","== compares, = assigns.","and, or, not combine conditions.","The : and 4-space indentation are mandatory."],"practice":[{"id":"l04p1","title":"Even or odd","prompt":"Read an integer n. Print Chẵn if n is divisible by 2, otherwise print Lẻ."},{"id":"l04p2","title":"Temperature rating","prompt":"Read a temperature t (integer). Print:\nLạnh if t < 18\nMát if 18 <= t <= 28\nNóng if t > 28"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which operator compares for equality?","options":["=","==","===","equals"]},{"q":"x = 7. What is the value of x > 5 and x < 10?","options":["True","False","7","Error"]},{"q":"If many elif branches are true, which one does Python run?","options":["All of them","The last one","The first true one","None of them"]}]},"l05":{"why":"Computers are best at doing the same thing again and again without getting tired. Printing 100 lines, adding 1000 numbers, going through a class list: all of it needs loops.","concept":"for i in range(n): repeats n times with i = 0, 1, ..., n-1. range(a, b) goes from a to b-1. range(a, b, buoc) steps by buoc.\n\nwhile dieu_kien: repeats while the condition stays true. You must have a statement that gradually makes the condition false, otherwise it loops forever (the grader stops after a few seconds).\n\nAccumulation pattern: create tong = 0 before the loop, each round tong = tong + gia_tri.\n\nbreak exits the loop immediately; continue skips the rest of the current round.","syntax":"for i in range(1, 6):\n    print(i)\n\nwhile n > 0:\n    n = n - 1","walkthrough":["Line 1: n = 5.","Line 2: tong starts at 0.","Line 3: i runs 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 (range stops before n + 1 = 6).","Line 4: each round adds i to tong: 1, 3, 6, 10, 15.","Line 5: printed after the loop (this line is NOT indented)."],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"for i in range(1, 5):  # want 1..5","why":"range stops before the last number, giving only 1..4.","fix":"range(1, 6) or range(1, n + 1)"},{"wrong":"while i < 5:\n    print(i)","why":"i never increases, so it loops forever.","fix":"Add i = i + 1 inside the loop."},{"wrong":"for i in range(5):\n    tong = 0\n    tong = tong + i","why":"Putting tong = 0 inside the loop wipes the total every round.","fix":"Put tong = 0 before the for loop."}],"summary":["for + range to repeat a known number of times.","while repeats by condition; remember to make the condition gradually false.","An accumulation variable is initialized before the loop.","break exits, continue skips the current round."],"practice":[{"id":"l05p1","title":"Multiplication table","prompt":"Read a number n. Print the multiplication table of n from 1 to 10, each line in the form: n x i = result\nFor example n = 3, the first line: 3 x 1 = 3"},{"id":"l05p2","title":"Sum of digits","prompt":"Read a positive integer n. Print the sum of the digits of n using a while loop (hint: n % 10 takes the last digit, n // 10 drops the last digit)."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which numbers does range(2, 8, 2) give?","options":["2 4 6 8","2 4 6","2 3 4 5 6 7","4 6 8"]},{"q":"Which statement exits a loop immediately?","options":["continue","stop","break","exit"]},{"q":"How many times does for i in range(3): run?","options":["2","3","4","Forever"]}]},"l06":{"why":"A class has 40 students, nobody creates 40 variables. A list holds a whole sequence of values in one variable and processes them with a loop.","concept":"A list is written in square brackets: diem = [8, 6, 9].\n\nIndices start at 0: diem[0] is 8. diem[-1] is the last element. Accessing a missing index causes IndexError.\n\nappend(x) adds x at the end. len(ds) is the number of elements. sum, max, min compute the total, largest, smallest.\n\ninput().split() splits one line into a list of strings; use [int(x) for x in input().split()] or list(map(int, input().split())) to get a list of numbers.","syntax":"ds = [1, 2, 3]\nds.append(4)\nprint(ds[0], len(ds))\nfor x in ds:\n    print(x)","walkthrough":["Line 1: split \"8 6 10\" into [\"8\", \"6\", \"10\"], convert each element to a number: [8, 6, 10].","Line 2: len gives 3.","Line 3: max gives 10.","Line 4: 24 / 3 = 8.0 (the / operator gives a real number)."],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"ds = [1, 2, 3]\nprint(ds[3])","why":"The list has 3 elements, indices only run 0 to 2: IndexError.","fix":"print(ds[2]) or ds[-1]"},{"wrong":"ds = input().split()\nprint(sum(ds))","why":"The elements are still strings, sum reports TypeError.","fix":"ds = list(map(int, input().split()))"},{"wrong":"ds.append(4, 5)","why":"append takes only one value.","fix":"ds.append(4) then ds.append(5), or ds.extend([4, 5])"}],"summary":["Lists in square brackets, indices from 0.","append, len, sum, max, min are everyday tools.","split() + map(int, ...) to read many numbers on one line."],"practice":[{"id":"l06p1","title":"Count positive numbers","prompt":"One line contains many integers separated by spaces. Print how many are greater than 0."},{"id":"l06p2","title":"Reverse a list","prompt":"One line contains words separated by spaces. Print the words in reverse order, separated by one space."}],"quiz":[{"q":"ds = [5, 7, 9]. What is ds[1]?","options":["5","7","9","Error"]},{"q":"Which statement adds an element to the end of a list?","options":["ds.add(x)","ds.push(x)","ds.append(x)","ds.insert(x)"]},{"q":"How much is len([1, [2, 3], 4])?","options":["3","4","2","Error"]}]},"l07":{"why":"Functions help split a large program into small, clearly named pieces that can be reused many times. This is the first step to writing code like a professional.","concept":"def ten_ham(tham_so): defines a function; return sends a result back to the caller. A function with no return returns None.\n\nDefault parameter: def chao(ten, loi=\"Xin chào\") lets you call chao(\"An\") or chao(\"An\", loi=\"Hi\").\n\nreturn a, b returns a tuple; receive it with x, y = ham().\n\nA variable created inside a function is local and disappears when the function ends. Pass data through parameters and return instead of using global variables.","syntax":"def ten_ham(a, b=10):\n    return a + b, a * b\n\ntong, tich = ten_ham(3)","walkthrough":["Lines 1-2: define the function thong_ke returning 3 values.","Line 4: call the function with [4, 8, 6]; the tuple (4, 8, 6.0) is unpacked into three variables.","Line 5: print the three values."],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"def tong(a, b):\n    print(a + b)\nx = tong(1, 2) + 1","why":"The function only prints, it does not return, so tong(1, 2) is None: TypeError when adding.","fix":"Use return a + b"},{"wrong":"def f(a=1, b):","why":"A default parameter must come after required parameters: SyntaxError.","fix":"def f(b, a=1):"},{"wrong":"def them(x, ds=[]):\n    ds.append(x)\n    return ds","why":"The default list is shared between calls, results accumulate unexpectedly.","fix":"def them(x, ds=None):\n    if ds is None:\n        ds = []"}],"summary":["def + return to create reusable functions.","Default parameters come after required parameters.","return a, b returns a tuple, receive with x, y = ...","Do not use a list as a default value."],"practice":[{"id":"l07p1","title":"Price function with discount","prompt":"Write a function tinh_tien(gia, so_luong, giam=0) returning gia * so_luong * (100 - giam) // 100.\nRead gia, so_luong, giam (one number per line) then print the function result."},{"id":"l07p2","title":"Function returning two values","prompt":"Write a function chia(a, b) returning two values: the integer quotient and the remainder. Read a, b on one line, print the quotient and remainder separated by a space."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does a function with no return give back?","options":["0","\"\"","None","Error"]},{"q":"def f(a, b=2): return a * b. What is f(5)?","options":["5","7","10","Error"]},{"q":"What type does return 1, 2 return?","options":["list","tuple","two separate values with no type","dict"]}]},"l08":{"why":"Phone contacts, scores by name, counting how often a word appears: 'key → value' data is everywhere. dict is the most used structure in real Python.","concept":"A dict is written {khoa: gia_tri}: diem = {\"An\": 8, \"Bình\": 7}. Get a value with diem[\"An\"]; assign diem[\"Chi\"] = 9 to add or change.\n\nA missing key causes KeyError; diem.get(\"Dũng\", 0) returns 0 instead of an error.\n\nLooping: for ten, d in diem.items(). \"An\" in diem checks whether a key exists.\n\nA set is a collection with no duplicates and no order: set([1, 2, 2]) is {1, 2}. Use sorted(set(...)) to print in order.","syntax":"d = {\"a\": 1}\nd[\"b\"] = 2\nfor k, v in d.items():\n    print(k, v)\ns = set([3, 1, 3])","walkthrough":["Line 1: split the sentence into a list of words.","Line 2: an empty dict for counting.","Lines 3-4: for each word, take the old count (default 0) and add 1.","Lines 5-6: loop over the alphabetically sorted keys and print."],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"dem[w] = dem[w] + 1","why":"The first time word w appears, the key does not exist yet: KeyError.","fix":"dem[w] = dem.get(w, 0) + 1"},{"wrong":"s = {}","why":"{} is an empty dict, not an empty set.","fix":"s = set()"},{"wrong":"print(set([3, 1, 2]))  # expecting order","why":"A set does not guarantee order when printed.","fix":"print(sorted(s))"}],"summary":["dict stores key → value, keys are unique.","get(k, mac_dinh) avoids KeyError.","items() to loop over both keys and values.","set removes duplicate elements."],"practice":[{"id":"l08p1","title":"Count characters","prompt":"Read a string (no spaces). Print each distinct character and its count, sorted by character, one line per character: ký_tự số_lần."},{"id":"l08p2","title":"Distinct numbers","prompt":"One line contains integers. Print the distinct numbers in increasing order, separated by spaces."}],"quiz":[{"q":"d = {\"x\": 1}. What does d.get(\"y\", 5) return?","options":["None","5","KeyError","1"]},{"q":"Which is the correct way to create an empty set?","options":["{}","[]","set()","()"]},{"q":"How much is len(set(\"hello\"))?","options":["5","4","3","Error"]}]},"l09":{"why":"Users will type wrong input: letters when a number is needed, division by 0. A good program does not 'crash' but reports the error clearly and keeps running.","concept":"Put code that may fail inside try:. If an error happens, Python jumps to the matching except instead of stopping the program.\n\nCatch specific errors: except ValueError (wrong type conversion), except ZeroDivisionError (division by 0). Do not use a bare except because it hides real errors.\n\nelse runs when try has no error; finally always runs (cleanup).\n\nraise ValueError(\"message\") reports an error yourself when data is invalid.","syntax":"try:\n    x = int(input())\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"Không phải số\")\nelse:\n    print(x * 2)","walkthrough":["Lines 1-2: a = 5, b = 0.","Line 4: 5 // 0 causes ZeroDivisionError, Python skips the rest of the try block.","Lines 5-6: the matching except branch, prints the message.","Lines 7-8: finally always runs."],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"try:\n    x = int(input())\nexcept:\n    pass","why":"A bare except swallows every error, even a mistyped variable name, making it very hard to find.","fix":"except ValueError: and report it to the user."},{"wrong":"try:\n    print(1/0)\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"lỗi\")","why":"Wrong error type: 1/0 is ZeroDivisionError, so it is not caught.","fix":"except ZeroDivisionError:"},{"wrong":"try\n    x = 1","why":"Missing the : after try.","fix":"try:"}],"summary":["try/except keeps a program from crashing.","Catch specific errors, avoid bare except.","else when there is no error, finally always runs.","raise to report an error yourself."],"practice":[{"id":"l09p1","title":"Safe number input","prompt":"Read one line. If it is an integer, print its square. If it is not an integer, print: Dữ liệu không hợp lệ"},{"id":"l09p2","title":"Division calculator","prompt":"Read two lines a and b. Print a / b rounded to 2 digits (use round). If b is 0 print: Lỗi chia cho 0. If the data is not a number print: Lỗi dữ liệu"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What error does int(\"abc\") cause?","options":["TypeError","ValueError","NameError","SyntaxError"]},{"q":"Which block always runs, error or not?","options":["else","except","finally","try"]},{"q":"Can try/except catch a SyntaxError inside the try block itself?","options":["Yes","No, because the code cannot run at all","Only with a bare except","Only with finally"]}]},"l10":{"why":"As programs grow, grouping related data and behaviour into one 'mould' makes code easier to understand and extend. Most Python libraries (Django, pandas...) are built on classes.","concept":"A class is a mould, an object is a product made from the mould. class TaiKhoan: defines the mould; tk = TaiKhoan(\"An\", 100) creates an object.\n\n__init__ runs when an object is created, to assign attributes: self.ten = ten.\n\nself is the very object being used; every method has self as its first parameter.\n\n__str__ returns the string used when printing the object.","syntax":"class TenClass:\n    def __init__(self, a):\n        self.a = a\n\n    def phuong_thuc(self):\n        return self.a\n\nx = TenClass(5)","walkthrough":["Lines 1-4: the TaiKhoan mould with two attributes chu, so_du.","Lines 6-7: the nap method adds money to the so_du of the very object (self).","Lines 9-10: __str__ decides how the object prints.","Line 12: create an object, __init__ runs with chu = \"An\", so_du = 100.","Lines 13-14: deposit 50 then print, Python calls __str__."],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"def nap(tien):\n    self.so_du += tien","why":"Missing self as the first parameter: calling tk.nap(50) reports TypeError.","fix":"def nap(self, tien):"},{"wrong":"def __init__(self, chu):\n    chu = chu","why":"Only assigns a local variable, the object gets no chu attribute.","fix":"self.chu = chu"},{"wrong":"def __int__(self, chu):","why":"Typing __int__ instead of __init__ means the constructor is never called.","fix":"def __init__(self, chu):"}],"summary":["A class is a mould, an object is a product.","__init__ assigns attributes through self.","Methods always have self first.","__str__ for readable object printing."],"practice":[{"id":"l10p1","title":"Rectangle","prompt":"Write a class HinhChuNhat with __init__(self, dai, rong), methods dien_tich() and chu_vi().\nRead dai, rong on one line, print the area and perimeter on two lines."},{"id":"l10p2","title":"Shopping cart","prompt":"Write a class GioHang with an empty item list when created, methods them(ten, gia) and tong().\nThe first line is the number of items n, the next n lines each contain: name price. Print the total."}],"quiz":[{"q":"When does __init__ run?","options":["When printing the object","When creating the object","When deleting the object","When calling any method"]},{"q":"What is self in a method?","options":["The class name","The object calling the method","A keyword that must be named self","A global variable"]},{"q":"Which method decides how print(doi_tuong) displays?","options":["__init__","__print__","__str__","show"]}]}};
